import{_ as t,o as a,c as r,an as s}from"./chunks/framework.DpyEUKpL.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"How the package fits together","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"design.md","filePath":"design.md","lastUpdated":null}'),i={name:"design.md"};function n(o,e,l,d,h,c){return a(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="How-the-package-fits-together" tabindex="-1">How the package fits together <a class="header-anchor" href="#How-the-package-fits-together" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How the package fits together {#How-the-package-fits-together}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>STAC.jl turns documents into values, then uses those same values for catalog traversal, API search, spatial selection, and asset opening. The design keeps three choices separate: what to parse, how to fetch it, and what to do with the result.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Layer</th><th style="text-align:right;">Owns</th><th style="text-align:right;">Source entry point</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Document model</td><td style="text-align:right;">Typed fields, extension values, metadata tails</td><td style="text-align:right;"><a href="https://github.com/asinghvi17/STAC.jl/blob/main/src/objects.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>objects.jl</code></a></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Parsing and writing</td><td style="text-align:right;">JSON ↔ document values</td><td style="text-align:right;"><a href="https://github.com/asinghvi17/STAC.jl/blob/main/src/document.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>document.jl</code></a>, <a href="https://github.com/asinghvi17/STAC.jl/tree/main/src/parse" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>parse/</code></a></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">IO</td><td style="text-align:right;">Bytes, requests, credentials, href resolution</td><td style="text-align:right;"><a href="https://github.com/asinghvi17/STAC.jl/tree/main/src/io" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>io/</code></a></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Traversal and search</td><td style="text-align:right;">Linked documents → pages → items</td><td style="text-align:right;"><a href="https://github.com/asinghvi17/STAC.jl/blob/main/src/traverse.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>traverse.jl</code></a>, <a href="https://github.com/asinghvi17/STAC.jl/tree/main/src/search" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>search/</code></a></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Spatial selection</td><td style="text-align:right;">Item positions that match a query</td><td style="text-align:right;"><a href="https://github.com/asinghvi17/STAC.jl/blob/main/src/spatialindex.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>spatialindex.jl</code></a></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Ecosystem adapters</td><td style="text-align:right;">Features, tables, rasters, bulk formats, plots</td><td style="text-align:right;"><a href="https://github.com/asinghvi17/STAC.jl/blob/main/src/geo.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>geo.jl</code></a>, <a href="https://github.com/asinghvi17/STAC.jl/blob/main/src/tables.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>tables.jl</code></a>, <a href="https://github.com/asinghvi17/STAC.jl/tree/main/ext" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer"><code>ext/</code></a></td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="Typed-fields-give-structure;-tails-preserve-unfamiliar-data" tabindex="-1">Typed fields give structure; tails preserve unfamiliar data <a class="header-anchor" href="#Typed-fields-give-structure;-tails-preserve-unfamiliar-data" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Typed fields give structure; tails preserve unfamiliar data {#Typed-fields-give-structure;-tails-preserve-unfamiliar-data}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Core fields have declared Julia types. Unrecognized JSON keys go into an ordered metadata dictionary called a <em>tail</em>. This lets the package offer predictable access without needing a new release for every producer-specific key.</p><p>An item has two tails: <code>properties.other</code> for property keys and <code>metadata</code> for top-level keys. Its <code>extensions</code> field holds the caller&#39;s eagerly selected extension structs. Known extension fields leave the property tail; other keys remain available for lookup and writing.</p><p>The writer reconstructs a JSON document from these parts. It restores discriminators, puts extension prefixes back, and omits the runtime origin. It writes the parsed values, not the source bytes: field order and timestamp spelling can change, typed timestamps retain only millisecond precision, and most typed null fields are omitted.</p><p>The <a href="./parsing">typed parsing page</a> explains how this split also supports static compilation.</p><h2 id="Documents-carry-an-origin;-callers-carry-the-transport" tabindex="-1">Documents carry an origin; callers carry the transport <a class="header-anchor" href="#Documents-carry-an-origin;-callers-carry-the-transport" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Documents carry an origin; callers carry the transport {#Documents-carry-an-origin;-callers-carry-the-transport}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A document&#39;s <code>href</code> says where it came from. Traversal resolves a relative link against that origin, fetches the child, and gives the child its own origin. The original link text stays intact.</p><p>The IO stack travels separately. The same document can therefore be read through a filesystem, HTTP, a cache, or a test fixture without changing its type or embedding credentials in it.</p><p>Origin belongs to the document, not every nested value. In particular, an asset does not inherit its item&#39;s origin: asset-opening code cannot resolve a relative asset href from the asset alone.</p><p>See <a href="./io">Fetching and credentials</a> for the public interface and relative-href limits.</p><h2 id="IO-wrappers-compose-policies" tabindex="-1">IO wrappers compose policies <a class="header-anchor" href="#IO-wrappers-compose-policies" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;IO wrappers compose policies {#IO-wrappers-compose-policies}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each wrapper implements a small protocol and delegates the remaining work. The default stack is a cache around a scheme router, with HTTP and path transports underneath. The router&#39;s tuple of children lets generated dispatch keep each transport type visible to the compiler.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Operation</th><th style="text-align:right;">Responsibility</th><th style="text-align:right;">Important boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>STAC.read(io, href)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Fetch bytes</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>CachingIO</code> caches this operation</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>STAC.request(io, method, href; headers, body)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Execute an API request</td><td style="text-align:right;">The cache forwards requests uncached, including GET</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>STAC.authfor(io, href)</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Find the applicable credentials</td><td style="text-align:right;">Wrappers must forward this too</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Credential discovery also serves external asset readers. Routing prepares a filename and driver configuration; the Rasters bridge passes those to GDAL. GDAL then reads the pixels itself. Sharing credentials does not mean sharing the STAC byte cache.</p><h2 id="Pages-unify-search;-backends-choose-when-to-work" tabindex="-1">Pages unify search; backends choose when to work <a class="header-anchor" href="#Pages-unify-search;-backends-choose-when-to-work" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Pages unify search; backends choose when to work {#Pages-unify-search;-backends-choose-when-to-work}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The search protocol centers on pages. A backend supplies pages of items; shared iteration flattens their features. An empty page can still have a successor, so flattening continues until page iteration ends.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Backend</th><th style="text-align:right;">First demand</th><th style="text-align:right;">Later demand</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">API</td><td style="text-align:right;">Fetch the first response page</td><td style="text-align:right;">Follow each response&#39;s <code>next</code> link</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Static catalog</td><td style="text-align:right;">Walk the catalog and materialize the filtered result</td><td style="text-align:right;">Slice the cached result into pages</td></tr></tbody></table><p>This gives both backends the same user-facing iteration interface without promising the same latency or memory use.</p><p>API pagination treats a <code>next</code> link as a request description. Its href, method, headers, body, and merge flag determine the next request. A merged body overlays the <strong>original search body</strong>. Keeping that logic at the page boundary prevents endpoint-specific cursor conventions from leaking into item iteration.</p><p>See <a href="./search">Searching</a> for query options and conformance checks.</p><h2 id="Spatial-selection-separates-candidates-from-exact-answers" tabindex="-1">Spatial selection separates candidates from exact answers <a class="header-anchor" href="#Spatial-selection-separates-candidates-from-exact-answers" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Spatial selection separates candidates from exact answers {#Spatial-selection-separates-candidates-from-exact-answers}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The index returns positions in the original item vector. Its tree stores manifold-specific bounding volumes: ordinary extents on the plane, XYZ boxes on the unit sphere. Spherical boxes make queries across the antimeridian possible without treating longitude as one continuous planar interval.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Query</th><th style="text-align:right;">Work performed</th><th style="text-align:right;">Result means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Plain extent or geometry</td><td style="text-align:right;">Tree overlap test</td><td style="text-align:right;">Bounding-volume candidates</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">DE-9IM predicate wrapper</td><td style="text-align:right;">Candidate selection, then geometry relation test</td><td style="text-align:right;">Matches for that predicate</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>Disjoint</code> predicate</td><td style="text-align:right;">Test every item with a geometry</td><td style="text-align:right;">Disjoint matches can lie outside overlap candidates</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Leaves prefer the item&#39;s bbox and fall back to a rectangle over geometry vertices. Exact tests use GeoInterface accessors and the index&#39;s manifold. These are separate responsibilities: a fast tree narrows the work; the predicate defines the answer.</p><p>See <a href="./spatial">Spatial selection</a> for runnable queries and null-geometry behavior.</p><h2 id="Adapters-keep-one-model-usable-across-packages" tabindex="-1">Adapters keep one model usable across packages <a class="header-anchor" href="#Adapters-keep-one-model-usable-across-packages" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Adapters keep one model usable across packages {#Adapters-keep-one-model-usable-across-packages}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>GeoInterface and Tables methods expose items as features and rows. Heavy integrations live in Julia package extensions under <code>ext/</code>, so core STAC use does not load raster, database, or plotting stacks.</p><p>There are two different meanings of <em>extension</em> here:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Kind</th><th style="text-align:right;">Purpose</th><th style="text-align:right;">Location</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">STAC extension struct, such as <code>STAC.EO</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Describe prefixed metadata fields</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>src/extensions/</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Julia package extension, such as <code>STACRastersExt</code></td><td style="text-align:right;">Add methods when dependencies load</td><td style="text-align:right;"><code>ext/</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>A new metadata schema belongs in the first category. A bridge to another package belongs in the second. For example, the Rasters bridge loads when both Rasters and ArchGDAL are present; it adapts existing assets rather than introducing another item model.</p><h2 id="Tests-check-the-boundaries-the-design-relies-on" tabindex="-1">Tests check the boundaries the design relies on <a class="header-anchor" href="#Tests-check-the-boundaries-the-design-relies-on" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Tests check the boundaries the design relies on {#Tests-check-the-boundaries-the-design-relies-on}&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:right;">Evidence</th><th style="text-align:right;">What it protects</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Vendored fixtures and round-trip tests</td><td style="text-align:right;">Parsing and reconstruction of real document shapes</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;"><code>FixtureIO</code> request replay</td><td style="text-align:right;">Production traversal and paging issue the expected method, href, and JSON body; unexpected requests fail locally</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">One safe testset per concern</td><td style="text-align:right;">Tests do not depend on another testset&#39;s imports or definitions</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Aqua with bridge dependencies loaded</td><td style="text-align:right;">Methods added by optional integrations remain compatible</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:right;">Linked trim programs</td><td style="text-align:right;">Selected read, traversal, search, and index paths remain statically resolvable</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Start a change at the layer that owns its policy, then test through its public boundary. Use <a href="./compilation">Static compilation</a> to distinguish paths that link today from paths whose unresolved calls are only budgeted.</p>',36)])])}const u=t(i,[["render",n]]);export{p as __pageData,u as default};
